(272359, 17742, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Hi SharQ...<br/> <br/>This is an excellent photo... The barren landscape with the tire tracks through the sand create a nice rhythmic flow from left to right in the image.  The texture in the sand also adds some nice minor contrast.  The clouds in this photo really seem to set it off for me.  Compositionally, the only thing that I think could make this image stronger would be to include more of the sky in a larger frame.  Maybe a portrait orientation with the same amount of ''land'' and two or three times as much sky would create even more impact.  The clouds are wonderful here and more of that would be good as well.  <br/> <br/>Have you experimented with this as a black and white yet?  If you do, I would love to see the result of that as well...<br/> <br/>Great work :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Hi SharQ... This is an excellent photo... The barren landscape with the tire tracks through the sand create a nice rhythmic flow from left to right in the image.  The texture in the sand also adds some nice minor contrast.  The clouds in this photo really seem to set it off for me.  Compositionally, the only thing that I think could make this image stronger would be to include more of the sky in a larger frame.  Maybe a portrait orientation with the same amount of ''land'' and two or three times as much sky would create even more impact.  The clouds are wonderful here and more of that would be good as well.   Have you experimented with this as a black and white yet?  If you do, I would love to see the result of that as well... Great work :) John Setzler', '2003-04-21 12:35:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(272391, 18201, 472, '<td valign="top">nope. seriously. im driving and i look up out my sunroof and squeezed off a bunch of shots.  the shutter speed was 1/4000 sec so it was sharp :)</td>', 'nope. seriously. im driving and i look up out my sunroof and squeezed off a bunch of shots.  the shutter speed was 1/4000 sec so it was sharp :)', '2003-04-21 13:00:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(272451, 18128, 3540, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>By Inspzil<br/> <br/>Composition - The branches frame this shot in beautifully.  I question the framing of this picture in general.  I think a landscape shot would be more advantageous to show a little more of the landscape and a little less of the clouds at the top of the frame.  Those are a bit grainy for some reason.  The sun is a little bit hot, but hey, it''s the sun for Pete''s sake.  There is a very calm feeling to this photo, even though the water is so choppy.  I think you did well with the composition of this photo, except for the orientation.<br/> <br/>Technical - These shots are tough.  I shoot a lot of sunrises and get caught up in the same type of scenario, with a hot sun that isn''t lighting everything else up proportionally.  So what I would''ve done is stopped this up to F6.3 or so and taken it again.  Normally on shots like this I manually bracket it and take it hot and stop up from there to make sure I have the right exposure.  I usually start where my exposure is a little higher than optimal and then turn the shutter faster every time I stop up.  I probably end up with at least 5 shots of the same exact thing with the exposures getting darker as I go.  Back to your shot.... I think that a higher F and shutter speed would''ve brought out the colors in the sky a lot better.  You wouldn''t really lose much detail from the branches or the rest of the landscape because they are pretty dark anyway.  Most of those would be captured in silhouette which is a nice look anyway.  It also might hide a little of the choppy water.<br/> <br/>Overall - This is a pretty good picture as is.  I think it wouldn''t take a lot to make it really good.  I like doing CC''s on pictures like this because it''s really up my alley, and its something that has a lot of potential and is worth the time to do these.  Hopefully this could be some help to you and good luck in the future. - Inspzil</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique ClubBy Inspzil Composition - The branches frame this shot in beautifully.  I question the framing of this picture in general.  I think a landscape shot would be more advantageous to show a little more of the landscape and a little less of the clouds at the top of the frame.  Those are a bit grainy for some reason.  The sun is a little bit hot, but hey, it''s the sun for Pete''s sake.  There is a very calm feeling to this photo, even though the water is so choppy.  I think you did well with the composition of this photo, except for the orientation. Technical - These shots are tough.  I shoot a lot of sunrises and get caught up in the same type of scenario, with a hot sun that isn''t lighting everything else up proportionally.  So what I would''ve done is stopped this up to F6.3 or so and taken it again.  Normally on shots like this I manually bracket it and take it hot and stop up from there to make sure I have the right exposure.  I usually start where my exposure is a little higher than optimal and then turn the shutter faster every time I stop up.  I probably end up with at least 5 shots of the same exact thing with the exposures getting darker as I go.  Back to your shot.... I think that a higher F and shutter speed would''ve brought out the colors in the sky a lot better.  You wouldn''t really lose much detail from the branches or the rest of the landscape because they are pretty dark anyway.  Most of those would be captured in silhouette which is a nice look anyway.  It also might hide a little of the choppy water. Overall - This is a pretty good picture as is.  I think it wouldn''t take a lot to make it really good.  I like doing CC''s on pictures like this because it''s really up my alley, and its something that has a lot of potential and is worth the time to do these.  Hopefully this could be some help to you and good luck in the future. - Inspzil', '2003-04-21 14:16:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(272465, 18269, 7151, '<td valign="top">Thanks everyone for the comments and compliments.  I was really kind of surprised that this was as well recieved as it was, just because I expected a lot of cloud pictures.  But I was happy with the shot myself.  I almost didn''t get it.  I had taken a lot of tighter shots, trying to get more detail on the hills.  Then, as I was ready to quit, my daughter asked if I had gotten those whispy (stratus?) clouds - this one shot was the one that worked.  Unfortunately, I had to crop a lot of the ground because below the bottom of what was left was a lot of messy looking development.  Obviously, from the comments, there was a mixture of opinions on how well that worked.  Thanks again.  BTW, the title turned out quite appropriate - the second storm that came in the next day dumped almost 3 inches of rain on us, which is a lot for So. Cal.</td>', 'Thanks everyone for the comments and compliments.  I was really kind of surprised that this was as well recieved as it was, just because I expected a lot of cloud pictures.  But I was happy with the shot myself.  I almost didn''t get it.  I had taken a lot of tighter shots, trying to get more detail on the hills.  Then, as I was ready to quit, my daughter asked if I had gotten those whispy (stratus?) clouds - this one shot was the one that worked.  Unfortunately, I had to crop a lot of the ground because below the bottom of what was left was a lot of messy looking development.  Obviously, from the comments, there was a mixture of opinions on how well that worked.  Thanks again.  BTW, the title turned out quite appropriate - the second storm that came in the next day dumped almost 3 inches of rain on us, which is a lot for So. Cal.', '2003-04-21 14:22:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(272475, 18133, 7187, '<td valign="top">This was my back yard after an ice storm.  That is my daughter in the picture and yes, I know she is smiling.  Those huge tree branches, suprisingly, didn''t even touch the swing set.  (Two smaller branches on each side of the slide supported the weight of the large branch.)  Anyway, it was hard to get a pic of her NOT smiling - she (and my other kids, and their friends) loved having that branch there to climb on. They were a little disappointed when we had the mess cleaned up.</td>', 'This was my back yard after an ice storm.  That is my daughter in the picture and yes, I know she is smiling.  Those huge tree branches, suprisingly, didn''t even touch the swing set.  (Two smaller branches on each side of the slide supported the weight of the large branch.)  Anyway, it was hard to get a pic of her NOT smiling - she (and my other kids, and their friends) loved having that branch there to climb on. They were a little disappointed when we had the mess cleaned up.', '2003-04-21 14:35:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(272482, 18134, 7365, '<td valign="top">This was about a mile from my home - in the neighborhood I used to live in, actually.  This was a garage (and there is a car in it, which didn''t show up in the photo).  The tree missed their house which is just to the right of the garage.  On the other side of their house (not shown), another [huge] tree fell completely over, uprooted and all.  It was quite a mess.  I took this picture from my van - this was the clearest shot I got.  It looked very clear on the display of my camera, so I thought it was a good picture.  It wasn''t until I got back home and saw it on the computer that I noticed the blurriness.  I decided to submit it anyway.</td>', 'This was about a mile from my home - in the neighborhood I used to live in, actually.  This was a garage (and there is a car in it, which didn''t show up in the photo).  The tree missed their house which is just to the right of the garage.  On the other side of their house (not shown), another [huge] tree fell completely over, uprooted and all.  It was quite a mess.  I took this picture from my van - this was the clearest shot I got.  It looked very clear on the display of my camera, so I thought it was a good picture.  It wasn''t until I got back home and saw it on the computer that I noticed the blurriness.  I decided to submit it anyway.', '2003-04-21 14:41:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(272636, 17848, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Hi Matt...<br/> <br/>This is an excellent capture.  It looks like something I would expect to stumble across in a museum of fine photography... I''m so impressed with this image that I don''t even feel qualified to ''critique'' it at all.  I see no flaw.  <br/> <br/>Maybe the tides are turning in the battle between good and evil.  The light of heaven is shining through :)<br/> <br/>Phenomenal shot...<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Hi Matt... This is an excellent capture.  It looks like something I would expect to stumble across in a museum of fine photography... I''m so impressed with this image that I don''t even feel qualified to ''critique'' it at all.  I see no flaw.   Maybe the tides are turning in the battle between good and evil.  The light of heaven is shining through :) Phenomenal shot... John Setzler', '2003-04-21 17:39:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(272644, 17912, 3301, '<td valign="top">Thanks for the comments on this photo. This was my first entry on DP. For the few questions I got... Yes, it had been raining (it had just stopped, or I wouldn''t have taken my brand new 10D out). No, this isn''t B&amp;W. If you look at the background you will see the coloring of the ground.<br/> <br/>Thanks again</td>', 'Thanks for the comments on this photo. This was my first entry on DP. For the few questions I got... Yes, it had been raining (it had just stopped, or I wouldn''t have taken my brand new 10D out). No, this isn''t B&W. If you look at the background you will see the coloring of the ground. Thanks again', '2003-04-21 17:47:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(272660, 17625, 205, '<td valign="top">should of got first</td>', 'should of got first', '2003-04-21 18:06:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(272673, 18201, 1031, '<td valign="top">I shoot lots of pictures while I''m driving around too. My camera won''t go to 1/4000 though...<br/> <br/>We took a somewhat similar approach to interpreting the challenge this week, but I shot mine out the car window while at a stoplight -- my sunroof leaks and is taped closed!</td>', 'I shoot lots of pictures while I''m driving around too. My camera won''t go to 1/4000 though... We took a somewhat similar approach to interpreting the challenge this week, but I shot mine out the car window while at a stoplight -- my sunroof leaks and is taped closed!', '2003-04-21 18:22:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(272677, 18128, 1031, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by inspzil:</b></div><hr/><i>Greetings from the Critique Club<br/>By Inspzil<br/> <br/>Composition - The branches frame this shot in beautifully.  I question the framing of this picture in general.  I think a landscape shot would be more advantageous to show a little more of the landscape and a little less of the clouds at the top of the frame...</i></td></tr></table>I think in the context of THIS challenge it was a good idea to include the differently-colored clouds at the top, even though a panoramic crop might make a better "sunset" picture overall.<br/> <br/>Did you know you can completely re-crop the version you post for printing? Customers will see a new preview generated from the printable file. It can still be associated with your entry, even if considerably adapted...</td>', 'Originally posted by inspzil:Greetings from the Critique ClubBy Inspzil Composition - The branches frame this shot in beautifully.  I question the framing of this picture in general.  I think a landscape shot would be more advantageous to show a little more of the landscape and a little less of the clouds at the top of the frame...I think in the context of THIS challenge it was a good idea to include the differently-colored clouds at the top, even though a panoramic crop might make a better "sunset" picture overall. Did you know you can completely re-crop the version you post for printing? Customers will see a new preview generated from the printable file. It can still be associated with your entry, even if considerably adapted...', '2003-04-21 18:31:08.000000', NULL, 0),
