(273750, 18240, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Hi Pep...<br/> <br/>This is a phenomenal shot... I love the ominous clouds and how they are put into perspective by the bridge in the distance...  I can''t really imagine improving this shot any.  It ''speaks'' quite well on its own.  I can''t really offer much critiqe here... <br/> <br/>Great shot :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Hi Pep... This is a phenomenal shot... I love the ominous clouds and how they are put into perspective by the bridge in the distance...  I can''t really imagine improving this shot any.  It ''speaks'' quite well on its own.  I can''t really offer much critiqe here...  Great shot :) John Setzler', '2003-04-22 18:22:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(273751, 18220, 6870, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>I concur with all of the compliments below!<br/> <br/>In this photo, I see first of all rain on the window, which is probably just what you want! The drops are in focus, but they do not prevent my eye from looking beyond to the landscape of the city. The cityscape is nice because it has a lot of interesting lines to keep my interest like the prevalent one going from the lower left almost up to the upper right. The staggered diagonal lines next to this main line is engaging, too. The rest of the image adds little details that keep the eye moving around, so it is an engaging picture. The whole image has a feeling of, well, scientific gloom(?): on the one hand, there is nothing there that really evokes any emotion, but the sky and rain do add a subtle sullen feel to the whole photo.<br/> <br/>To improve the shot, take the emotion you wanted to convey and see if you can enhance that through drama: try different points of view--closer perhaps to the raindrops, or a steeper angle or ????. The drama could also increase, perhaps by different lighting, taking the picture later in the evening, for instance. This is not intended to be a "DRAMATIC" image, for sure, but the feeling that you are trying to communicate could probably be enhanced. One comment below suggested moviing closer to the rain drops, and this may be, perhaps, the best option to explore, especially considering your title.<br/> <br/>You should feel pleased with this interesting image. Keep up the good work! I look forward to seeing your future images.<br/> <br/>Thanks,<br/> <br/>David</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! I concur with all of the compliments below! In this photo, I see first of all rain on the window, which is probably just what you want! The drops are in focus, but they do not prevent my eye from looking beyond to the landscape of the city. The cityscape is nice because it has a lot of interesting lines to keep my interest like the prevalent one going from the lower left almost up to the upper right. The staggered diagonal lines next to this main line is engaging, too. The rest of the image adds little details that keep the eye moving around, so it is an engaging picture. The whole image has a feeling of, well, scientific gloom(?): on the one hand, there is nothing there that really evokes any emotion, but the sky and rain do add a subtle sullen feel to the whole photo. To improve the shot, take the emotion you wanted to convey and see if you can enhance that through drama: try different points of view--closer perhaps to the raindrops, or a steeper angle or ????. The drama could also increase, perhaps by different lighting, taking the picture later in the evening, for instance. This is not intended to be a "DRAMATIC" image, for sure, but the feeling that you are trying to communicate could probably be enhanced. One comment below suggested moviing closer to the rain drops, and this may be, perhaps, the best option to explore, especially considering your title. You should feel pleased with this interesting image. Keep up the good work! I look forward to seeing your future images. Thanks, David', '2003-04-22 18:22:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(273785, 17680, 327, '<td valign="top"><b>CC....Greeting to you...ladpupmoe </b> <br/> <br/>Fits The Challenge-Yes <br/>Composition-Good <br/>Background-Good <br/>My Opinion-<i>text"I found that I had capture a snowflake in mid-air."</i> YES!! and really neat too. Good going!!! You could crop that down to just the snow flake. Interesting. Fun. I think the light is a bit bright for shooting snow...not easy. It''s really something you have to watch and be aware of. The colors are good as is the composition. The focus looks a bit off, but hey... ya don''t go around catching snowflakes in mid air every day. Shoot more.</td>', 'CC....Greeting to you...ladpupmoe   Fits The Challenge-Yes Composition-Good Background-Good My Opinion-text"I found that I had capture a snowflake in mid-air." YES!! and really neat too. Good going!!! You could crop that down to just the snow flake. Interesting. Fun. I think the light is a bit bright for shooting snow...not easy. It''s really something you have to watch and be aware of. The colors are good as is the composition. The focus looks a bit off, but hey... ya don''t go around catching snowflakes in mid air every day. Shoot more.', '2003-04-22 19:32:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(273796, 18260, 3320, '<td valign="top">FIRST IMPRESSION : Nice colors, summer but not specifically interesting shot.<br/> <br/>COMPOSITION : I like the arrangement of items and the colors are definitly summery : the skirt, the bikini. However the back ground is really too much. It''s probably a cover bed. I am not saying a white cover would have been better but .. something else, an other texture maybe. Like those ... I do not know how they are called ... like cheap rectangle or straw that you roll and that have a color border that are used to seat on the beach ? something like that.<br/> <br/>TECHNIQUE : Not oversharpened which I like the colors are nice and tnot too saturated which I like. Exposition is good except the whites of the Sun lotion however are blown out.<br/> <br/>FINAL IMPRESSION :<br/>I like the colors (except back ground) and to me that ''save'' the picture and it meets the challenge. I would have give that one a 5 for the idea and the colors (I did not vote that week). <br/> <br/>Lionel for the Critique Club<br/></td>', 'FIRST IMPRESSION : Nice colors, summer but not specifically interesting shot. COMPOSITION : I like the arrangement of items and the colors are definitly summery : the skirt, the bikini. However the back ground is really too much. It''s probably a cover bed. I am not saying a white cover would have been better but .. something else, an other texture maybe. Like those ... I do not know how they are called ... like cheap rectangle or straw that you roll and that have a color border that are used to seat on the beach ? something like that. TECHNIQUE : Not oversharpened which I like the colors are nice and tnot too saturated which I like. Exposition is good except the whites of the Sun lotion however are blown out. FINAL IMPRESSION :I like the colors (except back ground) and to me that ''save'' the picture and it meets the challenge. I would have give that one a 5 for the idea and the colors (I did not vote that week).  Lionel for the Critique Club', '2003-04-22 20:06:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(273797, 18094, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Hi Shutterbug...<br/> <br/>"As I walked out one evening,<br/>Walking down Bristol Street,<br/>The crowds upon the pavement<br/>Were fields of harvest wheat.<br/> <br/>And down by the brimming river<br/>I heard a lover sing<br/>Under an arch of the railway:<br/>''Love has no ending.''"<br/> <br/>- beginning of "As I walked Out One Evening" by W.H. Auden"<br/> <br/>I dunno why I thought of this poem when I saw this photo.  I guess it was just fresh in my mind, so I thought I would share :)<br/> <br/>The minor glint of sun still visible on the horizon really makes this photo pop nicely... I wonder if you managed to get a few more of it earlier in its setting cycle?  It''s a beautiful shot.. the cloud illumination is beautiful.<br/> <br/>The detail visible in the foreground water is also just perfect for this shot.  The only minor modifications I would suggest for this would be to give it a minor rotation to the left to level up the horizon just a bit.  It also would possibly be an improvement to crop off some of the black space along the bottom of the shot.  Ultimately, it could have been impressive to remove that part of the image by composing with the camera a little differently and include some more of the beautiful light in the sky :)<br/> <br/>Keep up the good work :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Hi Shutterbug... "As I walked out one evening,Walking down Bristol Street,The crowds upon the pavementWere fields of harvest wheat. And down by the brimming riverI heard a lover singUnder an arch of the railway:''Love has no ending.''" - beginning of "As I walked Out One Evening" by W.H. Auden" I dunno why I thought of this poem when I saw this photo.  I guess it was just fresh in my mind, so I thought I would share :) The minor glint of sun still visible on the horizon really makes this photo pop nicely... I wonder if you managed to get a few more of it earlier in its setting cycle?  It''s a beautiful shot.. the cloud illumination is beautiful. The detail visible in the foreground water is also just perfect for this shot.  The only minor modifications I would suggest for this would be to give it a minor rotation to the left to level up the horizon just a bit.  It also would possibly be an improvement to crop off some of the black space along the bottom of the shot.  Ultimately, it could have been impressive to remove that part of the image by composing with the camera a little differently and include some more of the beautiful light in the sky :) Keep up the good work :) John Setzler', '2003-04-22 20:07:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(273811, 18049, 3575, '<td valign="top">Sonja, what can I say. You''ve done it again. I can see why you didn''t run around bare foot - OUCH! Beautiful photograph.</td>', 'Sonja, what can I say. You''ve done it again. I can see why you didn''t run around bare foot - OUCH! Beautiful photograph.', '2003-04-22 20:23:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(273818, 17984, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Hi Mona...<br/> <br/>This is a beautiful photo.  It looks like a spring snow has subdued the new growth in your part of the world...  This lone flower seems to be struggling to find the light again and save itself from the freeze.  This is a great example of weather... even though it may be unwanted weather.  The water drops look like the represent the tears of this flower''s struggle for life...<br/> <br/>The square composition here seems to work very well, especially with the digaonal orientation of the flower in the frame.  Strong diagonals always seem to work well in a lot of compositions.  This composition also works very well because the purple flower is nicely isolated from any distracting elements.  It stands out and makes a bold statement... "I want to live!" :)<br/> <br/>Your photographic ''eye'' must be very strong, because this is a great capture.  Learning to take notice of things like this is a major part of creating great photos.  Technical skill with the camera is useless if you point your lens at a dull and boring subject.  Looking for the light... looking for the color... knowing how to find your opportunities... knowing when to look... all of these are part of the game :)<br/> <br/>Keep up the great work :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/> <br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Hi Mona... This is a beautiful photo.  It looks like a spring snow has subdued the new growth in your part of the world...  This lone flower seems to be struggling to find the light again and save itself from the freeze.  This is a great example of weather... even though it may be unwanted weather.  The water drops look like the represent the tears of this flower''s struggle for life... The square composition here seems to work very well, especially with the digaonal orientation of the flower in the frame.  Strong diagonals always seem to work well in a lot of compositions.  This composition also works very well because the purple flower is nicely isolated from any distracting elements.  It stands out and makes a bold statement... "I want to live!" :) Your photographic ''eye'' must be very strong, because this is a great capture.  Learning to take notice of things like this is a major part of creating great photos.  Technical skill with the camera is useless if you point your lens at a dull and boring subject.  Looking for the light... looking for the color... knowing how to find your opportunities... knowing when to look... all of these are part of the game :) Keep up the great work :) John Setzler  ', '2003-04-22 20:31:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(273850, 17937, 327, '<td valign="top"><b>CC......Hello Gina Rothfels</b> <br/> <br/>Fits The Challenge-Yes <br/>Composition-Good <br/>Background-Blue..very good <br/>My Opinion-I looked at this shot a long time. I like looking at it.<br/>I happen to read a short article about shooting into the sun. They listed several tricks, but mostly it was for macro. I''m attracted to this type of shot. It''s 90° different from something I''d of seen to shoot. [A bonus of being a member of DPC]I love that your eye saw this and captured it. I also like the patterns in the grates. The sun flare is also nice. Good job.<br/></td>', 'CC......Hello Gina Rothfels  Fits The Challenge-Yes Composition-Good Background-Blue..very good My Opinion-I looked at this shot a long time. I like looking at it.I happen to read a short article about shooting into the sun. They listed several tricks, but mostly it was for macro. I''m attracted to this type of shot. It''s 90° different from something I''d of seen to shoot. [A bonus of being a member of DPC]I love that your eye saw this and captured it. I also like the patterns in the grates. The sun flare is also nice. Good job.', '2003-04-22 21:38:53.000000', NULL, 0),
